00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.560
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: So this
is what's going on here.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.820
What we've done is, as we
described in last week's video,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.980
we had to create
a container first.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.830
So we used Cloud Build to
create a container that

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.410
then stored that container
in Container Registry.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.570
And then we used Terraform.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.940
We have, or I already
created, a whole bunch

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.520
of manifests that would
do all the steps required

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.780
by Terraform--

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.660
was going and
creating a whole bunch

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.590
of IAM permissions, service
enablement, and the like.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.320
It then created a Cloud Run
bucket and a whole bunch

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.400
of sample cats that we saw.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.630
It then created a function,
and then it created a service.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.519
What we have is a
service that goes

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.320
through all the
images in a bucket,

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.600
and for each of those
images, sends them off

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.080
to Cloud Functions and asks
Cloud Functions to talk

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.160
to the Vision API to get
information about each image.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.020
In this case, it's
a cat, and we're

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.040
asking it to describe the image.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.720
So it's coming back with
labels like cats, whiskers,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:40.710
and the like.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.200
Those results then
come back to Cloud Run,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.110
and then it displays all the
results in that lovely web

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.635
page.

00:04:47.635 --> 00:04:48.510
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.390
