00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.640
Can we do something about that?

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:06.710
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: We can.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.680
So what we'll do is we'll
do two things at once here.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.720
We'll get you to
reapply terraform again,

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.710
and we'll also get you
to define that variable.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:17.450
So if you go terraform apply,
but before you hit Enter,

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:29.540
go space, -var space, project=
and then your project name.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:30.830
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, I see.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:33.560
Let's do that.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:37.190
Now, this is a working
application here.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:40.520
Is it safe to run terraform
apply again when everything

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.930
is working as it should?

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.090
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Yep.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.870
In theory, Terraform
will not see any changes,

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.460
or any changes that it does
see are little tiny differences

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.890
in metadata or something
that it doesn't control.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.680
But whenever you
run terraform apply,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.500
you should just check
what's going to be applied.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:05.390
And then if you're comfortable
with those changes, type Y-E-S.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.440
MARTIN OMANDER:
Y-E-S. Let's do it.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:07.940
Boom.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.012
That was quick.

00:10:10.012 --> 00:10:12.470
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Would you
like to destroy everything now?

